@meta
id: 24-17
title: Recall the last command and widen it
chapter: 24
type: lesson
skills: cmdline-edit
par: 100
minutes: 6
vscode: replace one, then replace all
open: order.repository.ts

@brief
Every `findMany` in this repository has to return newest first. The
change on each call is the same: `findMany({ where` becomes
`findMany({ orderBy: { created: 'desc' }, where`. Three calls.

That substitute is long, and the first time you run a long substitute
you want to see it work on one line before you trust it with the file.
So do that. Put the cursor on the first `findMany`, run the substitute
on that line only, and look. Then bring the command back with `Ctrl-P`,
jump to the front of it with `Ctrl-B`, put `%` in front of the `s`, and
run it again on the whole file. You typed the long part once.

@keys
| `Ctrl-P` | on the command line: previous command from history |
| `Ctrl-N` | the next one |
| `Ctrl-B` | cursor to the beginning of the command line |
| `Ctrl-E` | cursor to the end |
| `Ctrl-W` | delete the word before the cursor |
| `Ctrl-U` | delete everything before the cursor |

@hints
`/findMany` gets you to the first call. `:s/.../.../` on that line
first, no range. Check the result.
--
Press `:` and then `Ctrl-P`. The previous command is back, cursor at
its end. `Ctrl-B` puts the cursor before the `s`. Type `%`, press
Enter.
--
`:s/findMany({ where/findMany({ orderBy: { created: 'desc' }, where/`
once, then `:` `Ctrl-P` `Ctrl-B` `%` Enter.

@solution
    /findMany<Enter>
    :s/findMany({ where/findMany({ orderBy: { created: 'desc' }, where/<Enter>
    : <C-p> <C-b> % <Enter>
    :wq<Enter>

Eighty-seven keystrokes, and sixty-nine of them are the substitute,
typed once.

The command line is an editable line of text, and these are its editing
keys. `Ctrl-B` and `Ctrl-E` are the ends. `Ctrl-W` eats a word backward,
`Ctrl-U` eats everything backward. `Ctrl-P` and `Ctrl-N` walk history,
and if you have typed a few characters first they walk only the history
entries that start with those characters, so `:s` `Ctrl-P` finds the
last substitute and skips the `:w`s in between.

Try-on-one-line-then-widen is the habit. It costs three keystrokes and
it means you never run a wrong substitute over a whole file, undo it,
and try to remember what you typed. The command line remembers.

What the command line cannot do is move by a word or change something
in the middle. Notice that `Ctrl-W` only works backward from the
cursor. For a fix in the middle of a long command you would have to
`Ctrl-U` and retype, and the next lesson is the way out of that.

@start order.repository.ts
import { injectable } from 'tsyringe';
import { Order as OrderRecord } from '@prisma/client';
import { BaseRepository } from '../../infrastructure/base.repository';
import { Order } from './order.entity';

@injectable()
export class OrderRepository extends BaseRepository<OrderRecord, Order> {
  public async findByCustomer(args: { customerId: string }) {
    const records = await this.prisma.order.findMany({ where: { customerId: args.customerId, deleted: null } });

    return records.map((record) => this.toEntity(record));
  }

  public async findByStatus(args: { status: string }) {
    const records = await this.prisma.order.findMany({ where: { status: args.status, deleted: null } });

    return records.map((record) => this.toEntity(record));
  }

  public async findCancelled() {
    const records = await this.prisma.order.findMany({ where: { status: 'CANCELLED', deleted: null } });

    return records.map((record) => this.toEntity(record));
  }
}

@expected order.repository.ts
import { injectable } from 'tsyringe';
import { Order as OrderRecord } from '@prisma/client';
import { BaseRepository } from '../../infrastructure/base.repository';
import { Order } from './order.entity';

@injectable()
export class OrderRepository extends BaseRepository<OrderRecord, Order> {
  public async findByCustomer(args: { customerId: string }) {
    const records = await this.prisma.order.findMany({ orderBy: { created: 'desc' }, where: { customerId: args.customerId, deleted: null } });

    return records.map((record) => this.toEntity(record));
  }

  public async findByStatus(args: { status: string }) {
    const records = await this.prisma.order.findMany({ orderBy: { created: 'desc' }, where: { status: args.status, deleted: null } });

    return records.map((record) => this.toEntity(record));
  }

  public async findCancelled() {
    const records = await this.prisma.order.findMany({ orderBy: { created: 'desc' }, where: { status: 'CANCELLED', deleted: null } });

    return records.map((record) => this.toEntity(record));
  }
}
